    {
      "id": "X5PWnJoK-s2Yp7kdblRBc",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 6810.531434974122,
      "y": -379.2604637618924,
      "width": 799.8193359375,
      "height": 1975,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2J",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 138311547,
      "version": 346,
      "versionNonce": 1298348113,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788164191288,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Q: Why do you need AI? \nBecause raw imagery and point clouds do not contain ready-made cadastral\nsemantics. AI can learn to detect and classify buildings and structural\nfeatures at scale, reducing manual extraction.\n\n Q: Is your whole AI Engine actually AI? \nNo. It is hybrid. AI is used for perception-heavy tasks; geometry\nperforms 3D construction; GIS performs deterministic topology checks;\nthe ULPIN service generates a stable identifier.\n\n Q: What exactly does the model output?\n Usually semantic labels, probabilities, masks or point-wise class\npredictions. Those outputs are converted into spatial geometry. \n\nQ: Why not use only GIS? \nGIS stores and analyzes spatial objects well, but raw images and\nunclassified point clouds still need interpretation. AI supplies that\nperception layer. Q: Why not use only AI? Cadastral geometry and\nidentifiers need deterministic, explainable rules. AI is best used for\nperception, not as the sole authority for final property boundaries.\n\n Q: Why PointNet++? \nIt is designed for hierarchical feature learning on point sets and\ncaptures local geometric context better than a simple point-wise model.\n\n Q: Why U-Net? \nIt is a well-understood encoder-decoder architecture for dense\nsegmentation and is a practical baseline for building footprint\nextraction. \n\nQ: Why RANSAC? \nRANSAC is robust to outliers and is useful for fitting planes such as\ncandidate floor/ceiling surfaces in noisy point clouds. \n\nQ: Why Shapely/PostGIS? \nTopology is a deterministic spatial-rule problem. These tools provide\nreproducible geometric and spatial predicates.\n\n Q: Why not AI for ULPIN? \nSIH26011 – 3D ULPIN | AI Engine Research ReportIdentifiers should be\ndeterministic and reproducible. AI randomness is unnecessary and\nundesirable for identity generation. \n\nQ: What if the AI makes a mistake? \nUse confidence thresholds, independent geometric validation, multi-\nsource evidence and human/surveyor verification before final\nregistration. \n\nQ: Where does training data come from? \nUse public labelled geospatial datasets where licensing and task fit\npermit, plus synthetic labelled buildings for the PoC. The exact final\ndataset depends on the selected model. \n\nQ: Are you training from scratch? \nPrefer transfer learning or a pretrained model for the PoC, then fine-\ntune if task-specific data is available. Training from scratch is\nusually unnecessary for a hackathon prototype. \n\nQ: How do you measure success? \nUse IoU/F1/precision/recall for segmentation, boundary/height/volume\nerror for geometry, conflictdetection accuracy for topology, and end-to-\nend processing time. \n\nQ: What is the difference between your system and a 3D city model? \nA 3D city model can visualize buildings. Our focus is the cadastral\nlayer: individual vertical property volumes, validated relationships,\nidentifiers and associated property records. \n\nQ: Can you legally determine ownership using AI? \nThe AI does not decide legal ownership. It creates and validates spatial\nrepresentations that can be linked to authoritative ownership records.\nLegal authority remains with the competent cadastral/land-record\nprocess. \n\nQ: Can your system detect every underground utility? \nNo. Underground detection depends strongly on sensing quality and field\nconditions. The prototype should present subsurface AI as assisted\ndetection/classification with validation, not universal automatic\ndiscovery.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Q: Why do you need AI? \nBecause raw imagery and point clouds do not contain ready-made cadastral semantics. AI can learn to detect and classify buildings and structural features at scale, reducing manual extraction.\n\n Q: Is your whole AI Engine actually AI? \nNo. It is hybrid. AI is used for perception-heavy tasks; geometry performs 3D construction; GIS performs deterministic topology checks; the ULPIN service generates a stable identifier.\n\n Q: What exactly does the model output?\n Usually semantic labels, probabilities, masks or point-wise class predictions. Those outputs are converted into spatial geometry. \n\nQ: Why not use only GIS? \nGIS stores and analyzes spatial objects well, but raw images and unclassified point clouds still need interpretation. AI supplies that perception layer. Q: Why not use only AI? Cadastral geometry and identifiers need deterministic, explainable rules. AI is best used for perception, not as the sole authority for final property boundaries.\n\n Q: Why PointNet++? \nIt is designed for hierarchical feature learning on point sets and captures local geometric context better than a simple point-wise model.\n\n Q: Why U-Net? \nIt is a well-understood encoder-decoder architecture for dense segmentation and is a practical baseline for building footprint extraction. \n\nQ: Why RANSAC? \nRANSAC is robust to outliers and is useful for fitting planes such as candidate floor/ceiling surfaces in noisy point clouds. \n\nQ: Why Shapely/PostGIS? \nTopology is a deterministic spatial-rule problem. These tools provide reproducible geometric and spatial predicates.\n\n Q: Why not AI for ULPIN? \nSIH26011 – 3D ULPIN | AI Engine Research ReportIdentifiers should be deterministic and reproducible. AI randomness is unnecessary and undesirable for identity generation. \n\nQ: What if the AI makes a mistake? \nUse confidence thresholds, independent geometric validation, multi-source evidence and human/surveyor verification before final registration. \n\nQ: Where does training data come from? \nUse public labelled geospatial datasets where licensing and task fit permit, plus synthetic labelled buildings for the PoC. The exact final dataset depends on the selected model. \n\nQ: Are you training from scratch? \nPrefer transfer learning or a pretrained model for the PoC, then fine-tune if task-specific data is available. Training from scratch is usually unnecessary for a hackathon prototype. \n\nQ: How do you measure success? \nUse IoU/F1/precision/recall for segmentation, boundary/height/volume error for geometry, conflictdetection accuracy for topology, and end-to-end processing time. \n\nQ: What is the difference between your system and a 3D city model? \nA 3D city model can visualize buildings. Our focus is the cadastral layer: individual vertical property volumes, validated relationships, identifiers and associated property records. \n\nQ: Can you legally determine ownership using AI? \nThe AI does not decide legal ownership. It creates and validates spatial representations that can be linked to authoritative ownership records. Legal authority remains with the competent cadastral/land-record process. \n\nQ: Can your system detect every underground utility? \nNo. Underground detection depends strongly on sensing quality and field conditions. The prototype should present subsurface AI as assisted detection/classification with validation, not universal automatic discovery.",
